r>2002</year><volume>30</volume><fpage>475</fpage><lpage>481</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11788710</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b69"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>AG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Hepatic steatosis in transgenic mice overexpressing human histone deacetylase 1</article-title><source>Biochem. Biophys. Res. Commun.</source><year>2005</year><volume>330</volume><fpage>461</fpage><lpage>466</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15796905</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b70"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Molecular dissection of formation of senescence-associated heterochromatin foci</article-title><source>Mol. Cell. Biol.</source><year>2007</year><volume>27</volume><fpage>2343</fpage><lpage>2358</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17242207</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b71"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Formation of MacroH2A-containing senescence-associated heterochromatin foci and senescence driven by ASF1a and HIRA</article-title><source>Dev. Cell</source><year>2005</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>19</fpage><lpage>30</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15621527</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b72"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zweidler</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Resolution of histones by polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis in presence of nonionic detergents</article-title><source>Methods Cell Biol.</source><year>1978</year><volume>17</volume><fpage>223</fpage><lpage>233</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">703614</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary material</title><p>The following supplementary material is available for this article:</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD1"><label>Fig S1</label><caption><p>Irreversibility of the senescent state induced by HDAC1. (A) Induction of HDAC1 protein (see Fig. 1B) results in increased HDAC1 activity. (B) HDAC1 overexpression results in progressive loss of DNA synthesis. (C) Short-term induction of HDAC1 does not cause growth arrest. UCD-HDAC1 cells were seeded in p35mm plastic dishes, left untreated, treated with 2 g &#x003bc;g mL<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> Dox to induce HDAC1 for 2 days, or treated with Dox for 2 days followed by removal of the drug for 8 days. SA-&#x003b2;-gal staining (Dimri et al., 1995) was negative in all three conditions. Removal of Dox allowed the cells to proliferate without evident signs of growth alterations or phenotypic changes in their morphology. (D) Long-term induction results in irreversible growth arrest and senescence. Cells treated with Dox for 3 weeks displayed large cells with transparent cytoplasm and were SA-&#x003b2;-gal-positive. Removal of Dox for 8 days did not result in changes of the flat morphology or induce significant proliferation. The cells were fixed with formaldehyde and stained with Crystal violet. (E) The telomerase repeat amplification protocol (TRAP) previously described (Suwa et al., 2001) was used to measure telomerase activity before and after induction of HDAC1 in UCD cells. Two different primary cultures of human normal proliferating melanocytes [NHM (1) and NHM(2)] were used as negative controls.</p></caption><media xlink:href="ace0006-0577-S1.eps" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c819e0N0x32b87b0" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="postscript"/><p><bold>Experimentals procedures</bold></p><p><bold>HDAC1 Activity Assay.</bold> Cell extracts from UCD-wild-type or UCD-HDAC1 were used to determine RB-associated HDAC activity (HDAC assay kit; Upstate). A SGRGKGGKGLGKGGAKRHRKVLR peptide substrate was prelabeled with [3H]-sodium acetate using benzotriazol-1-yloxytris-(dimethylamino)-phosphonium hexafluoruphosphate. The immunoprecipitates were incubated with the labeled peptide overnight at room temperature. Radioactivity released by the labeled peptide (HDAC activity) was measured after ethyl-acetate extraction.</p><p><bold>BrdU labeling.</bold> The BrdU activity assay was performed using a commercially available kit from Roche Applied Sciences. Briefly, UCD-HDAC1 and UCD wild-type cells were seeded on coverslips and treated with or without 2 &#x003bc;g mL<sup>&#x02212;1</sup> doxycyclin for the time indicated in the figures. Afterwards, the cells were incubated with 1 : 1000 dilution of BrdU in the fresh medium and the percent of positive cells determined by immunocytochemistry using an anti-BrdU monoclonal antibody provided by the manufacturer.</p><p><bold>References</bold></p><p>Dimri GP, Lee X, Basile G, Acosta M, Scott G, Roskelley C, Medrano EE, Linskens M, Rubelj I, Pereira-Smith O (1995) A biomarker that identifies senescent human cells in culture and in aging skin <italic>in vivo</italic>. <italic>Proc Natl Acad Sci. USA</italic>, <bold>92</bold>, 9363&#x02013;9367.</p><p>Suwa T, Yang L, and Hornsby PJ (2001) Telomerase activity in primary cultures of normal adrenocortical cells. <italic>J Endocrinol.</italic> <bold>170</bold>, 677&#x02013;684.</p></supplementary-material><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD2"><label>Fig S2</label><caption><p>Normal human senescent melanocytes display increased HP1&#x003b2;. Left panel: proliferating melanocytes display nucleoplasmic distribution of HP1&#x003b2;. Right panel: HP1&#x003b2; is mostly redistributed in nuclear foci in senescent cells.</p></caption><media xlink:href="ace0006-0577-S2.eps" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1c819e0N0x32b8870" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="postscript"/><p><bold>Experimentals procedures</bold></p><p>Cells were fixed with 10% formaldehyde, washed with PBS containing 0.05% Tween 20 (PBS-T), and permeabilized in 1% Triton X-100 in PBS for 20 min. After incubation with a blocking buffer (5% bovine serum albumin in PBS), the cells were exposed to anti-HP1&#x003b2; antibody (monoclonal antibody 3448, CHEMICON) followed by incubation with Alexa Fluor Alexa Fluor 488 (Invitrogen; Molecular Probes, Eugene, OR, USA) secondary antibodies. After a final wash, the slides were mounted with antifade mounting media containing DAPI (H-1200, Vector Laboratories) and observed with an inverted fluorescence microscope.</p><p>This material is available as part of the online article from: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.blackwell-synergy.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1474-9726.2007.00308.x">http://www.blackwell-synergy.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1474-9726.2007.00308.x</ext-link> (This link will take you to the article abstract).</p><p>Please note: Blackwell Publishing are not responsible for the content or functionality of any supplementary materials supplied by the authors. Any queries (other than missing material) should be directed to the corresponding author for the article.</p></supplementary-material></sec></back></article>